Osgood-Schlatter Disease
Young Adult & Adolecence
Anterior knee pain, Tibial tuberosity prominence, Point tenderness, Pain on resisted extension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-031
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of anterior knee pain rendering the patient unable to weight-bear following a sporting event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, severe inability to bear weight on the right leg, accompanied by intense localized knee pain and swelling following an acute physical stressor. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture, septic arthritis, or ligamentous rupture, and to provide immediate clinical stabilization prior to the availability of the patient's regular GP.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I came down hard after a jump and my knee feels like it was ripped off; I can't put any weight on it at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe, acute exacerbation of an insidious onset anterior knee pain that has been intermittently present for 3 months, typically exacerbated by running or jumping. During a basketball game 4 hours ago, the patient experienced a sharp, intense increase in pain localized just below the kneecap after landing a jump. The patient notes immediate, localized swelling and marked prominence of the tibial tuberosity. The severe pain has resulted in a profoundly antalgic gait and an inability to independently ambulate. The patient reports severe tightness of the quadriceps and hamstring musculature prior to the event. The pain is somewhat relieved by strict rest and elevation.
Functional Status: Bedbound; requires physical support from a parent to ambulate to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding a potential season-ending injury or requirement for surgery.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/75 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, guarding the right lower extremity, and refusing to actively move the knee.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination of the right knee reveals marked localized swelling and bony prominence of the tibial tuberosity. There is exquisite point tenderness directly over the tibial tuberosity upon light palpation. Crucially, there is an absence of joint effusion, and ligamentous testing (Lachman, Anterior/Posterior Drawer, Varus/Valgus stress) demonstrates no instability. The severe anterior knee pain is intensely reproduced by resisted knee extension. The clinical presentation firmly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of anterior knee pain, tibial tuberosity prominence, point tenderness, and pain on resisted extension.
Environmental Observations: Ice packs and a compression bandage are present but applied incorrectly over the patella rather than the tuberosity.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Ibuprofen 400mg 2 hours ago with minimal relief of the acute mechanical pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Exacerbation of Osgood-Schlatter Disease (Suspected Micro-avulsion) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Complete Tibial Tuberosity Avulsion Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the intact active extension mechanism (patient can straight-leg raise with difficulty) and absence of a palpable high-riding patella, though formal X-ray is required.

Patellar Tendonitis (Jumper's Knee) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain strictly localizing to the bony tibial tuberosity insertion rather than the inferior pole of the patella.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, the lack of a generalized tense joint effusion, and the purely mechanical mechanism of the acute pain flare.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic localized mechanical signs of severe Osgood-Schlatter disease, acutely exacerbated by a high-impact jumping mechanism. The severe pain and inability to weight-bear necessitated an immediate after-hours home visit to safely exclude a complete surgical avulsion fracture of the tibial tuberosity or an acute intra-articular injury. Clinical examination confirmed an intact extensor mechanism and localized the pathology firmly to the apophysis, averting an unnecessary emergency department presentation for an acute surgical consult. Immediate intervention focused on mechanical offloading, targeted cryotherapy, and arranging for a non-urgent confirmatory radiograph to rule out significant fragmentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided clinical reassurance regarding the likely benign, self-limiting nature of the apophysitis. Instructed strict non-weight-bearing with crutches for 48 hours. Ordered a non-urgent lateral plain radiograph of the knee via the regular GP to confirm expected fragmentation or irregular ossification of the tibial tuberosity and exclude a major avulsion fracture.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce the acute mechanical stress on the inflamed bone, utilize crutches for all ambulation over the next 48 hours, directly removing the contractile force of the quadriceps that is pulling on the injured area. To effectively reduce the localized swelling, apply an ice pack specifically to the bony bump below the knee, not the kneecap itself, targeting the exact site of inflammation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Crutches provided from the clinical vehicle.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to review the X-ray and arrange a referral to a sports physiotherapist for targeted hamstring/quadriceps stretching.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the knee swells massively, becomes hot and red, if the patient develops a fever, or if they suddenly become completely unable to lift their straight leg off the bed.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically presumed as an acute flare of Osgood-Schlatter disease; an X-ray is required to ensure a large piece of bone has not broken off.
Clinical Course: The acute, severe pain will settle with 48 hours of strict rest, but the underlying ache will persist intermittently until you finish growing and the growth plate fuses.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if your knee joint itself swells up like a balloon, becomes hot to the touch, or if you completely lose the ability to straighten your leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The large muscle on the front of your thigh attaches to the bone just below your knee. Because you are growing rapidly and playing high-impact sports, that muscle is pulling so hard it is causing micro-tears and inflammation at the attachment point on the bone.
Actionable Steps: Use the crutches to completely rest the leg. Apply ice directly to the painful bony bump for 15 minutes every two hours. Do not attempt to run or jump until cleared by your physiotherapist, as this will only rip the healing tissue again.

